Data Writing Modes in Apache Spark
When writing data in Spark, you specify a save mode to control behavior when the destination already exists. There are four modes:

1. overwrite
Deletes existing data and writes new data.
pythondf.write.mode("overwrite").parquet("/path/to/output")
 Destructive — existing data is permanently removed.

2. append
Adds new data to existing data without touching what's already there.
pythondf.write.mode("append").parquet("/path/to/output")
Useful for incremental loads or streaming sinks.

3. ignore
Does nothing if data already exists — silently skips the write.
pythondf.write.mode("ignore").parquet("/path/to/output")
Behaves like CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS in SQL.

4. errorIfExists (default)
Throws an error if the destination already has data.
pythondf.write.mode("errorIfExists").parquet("/path/to/output")
This is the default behavior if you don't specify a mode.

